  "query_preview": "..A cold front has moved through the CWA with\nlingering mountain snow remaining across areas that do well in\nnorthwest flow. This period of northwest flow hasn't been as\nefficient as some of the guidance suggested yesterday so only\nanother inch or two of snow is expected across the terrain before\ndrier air starts to move into the region. \n\nRidging will quickly build into the area later tonight and persist\nthrough Sunday and Monday. The coldest temperatures of the season\nwill develop overnight tonight with most locations, outside of\nlower Washington county getting near or below freezing. High\ntemperatures will warm back up 3-8 degrees above normal Sunday and\nMonday before another negatively tilted trough moves in for\nTuesday.\n\nThe 700 mb temperatures associated with the trough on Tuesday have\ntrended slightly warmer in the most recent guidance. This will\nlimit the valley snow probabilities with current snow levels\ngetting down to 5500-6000 feet by Wednesday morning before\nconditions start to dry. This trough looks very similar to the one\nthat moved through today. It will be a fast moving trough with a\ncold front that will bring widespread valley rain and mountain\nsnow to the entire CWA with the highest totals favored across the\nsouthern mountains. Snow amounts will be between 3-8 inches with\n5-10 inches across the southern mountains.\n\nThe Thursday/Friday storm continues to have little run-to-run\nconsistency. ~60% of ensemble members bring a closed low through\nthe area while the other 40% of members have a more progressive\nopen wave. This system has also trended slightly warmer over the\nlast several model runs which will decrease the potential for\nvalley snowfall.",
